# Dependencies
node_modules/
.venv/
env/
venv/
.wrangler/

# Python. The setuptools build-output names are ROOT-ANCHORED on purpose: they
# mean "a build directory at the top of the repo", and unanchored they match at
# every depth. `lib/` in particular silently swallowed pwa/lib/ — the vendored
# WGo.js / JSZip / math.js bundles — which only stayed in the repo because they
# were already tracked when the rule arrived. New files there were dropped with
# no error, the way a .gitignore miss always fails.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
/build/
/develop-eggs/
/dist/
/downloads/
/eggs/
/lib/
/lib64/
/parts/
/sdist/
/var/
/wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# Build outputs
/pwa/dist/
/pwa/wgo.js/

# Deployed tsumego zips: regenerable via pipeline/package_for_pwa.py from the
# raw SGFs in tsumegos/ + embeddings + ratings. The expensive-to-regenerate
# inputs stay in the repo; the packaged outputs do not.
/pwa/tsumegos/
# lookup.txt is another package_for_pwa output (tsumego_id -> zipHash -> size),
# copied into the gitignored dist/ at build time. Useless without the (ignored)
# zips it indexes, so it's regenerated, not tracked.
/pwa/lookup.txt

# IDEs and OS
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Claude Code harness runtime state (which session holds the scheduled-tasks
# lock); rewritten every session. settings*.json stay tracked.
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# Logs
*.log

models/

# Bot executables and their weight blobs: build artifacts of upstream projects,
# not authored content, and katago_cuda sat within 1 MiB of GitHub's 100 MiB
# hard per-blob limit. Fetch/build them yourself; KATAGO_PATH (utils/
# katago/controller.py) points at bots/katago by default. analysis.cfg stays
# TRACKED — pipeline.tune_engine rewrites it in place per GPU, so it is state,
# not an artifact.
/bots/*
!/bots/analysis.cfg

# One-time 2026-07-21 id-surgery working directory (id map, staging SQL, the
# pre-change DB backup). The surgery is done and its scripts take every path as
# an explicit argument, so nothing here is an input to the pipeline.
/surgery/

# Malformed problems quarantined by auxiliary/corpus_surgery.py. Never read
# back by the pipeline (the surgery scripts only ever WRITE here, recreating
# the directory on demand), so there is nothing to version.
/broken/
/tsumegos/broken/
# auxiliary/add_losing_lines --out-dir: repaired copies waiting to be merged
# back over tsumegos/<size>/. Transient, and regenerable by re-running.
/tsumegos/fixed/

# Pipeline-extracted activations: per-cell trunk vectors, ~tens of GB at fp16,
# regenerable via pipeline/extract_activations.py. Not for version control.
activations/

# Pre-projected per-cell K-dim axis vectors, one npz per problem. Written by
# pipeline/build_axes.py, consumed by pipeline/package_for_pwa.py. Reproducible
# from activations/.
axes/per_cell/

# Skill-axis fit outputs: regenerable from activations/, multi-MB, and they churn
# on every refit. TWO files in here stay TRACKED on purpose, both of them things a
# refit CANNOT reproduce — labels.json (the shipping decision in `components`, plus
# the dev motif store's names and provenance) and label_anchors.npz (that store's
# directions). Everything else regenerates:
#
#   motif_proxies.npz  the directions pipeline.train_mirt teaches its axes onto.
#                      Untracked because it IS reproducible — the rules live in
#                      utils/rating/motif_rules.py and the labelling is a pure
#                      function of them and the corpus, so the committed rule is
#                      the durable artifact and the npz is a cache of it. It also
#                      changes shape with the corpus, which is why a direction here
#                      cannot be content-hashed the way a stored one is.
#   axes.npz           every run rewrites it, and it carries the embedding_version
#                      packaging stamps, so a committed copy is a stale version
#                      waiting to be shipped onto some other fit's embeddings.
axes/per_problem.csv
axes/motif_proxies.npz
axes/axes.npz

# Local snapshot of the REMOTE production D1: a large (~50 MB) dump pulled by
# pipeline.fetch_database. Regenerable on demand and changes constantly, so it is
# not version-controlled. d1.sqlite is the canonical binary (query this one);
# d1.sql is the text dump regenerated from it. It sat under attempts/ until
# 2026-08-03, next to the TRACKED per-size bot benchmark results — those are a
# pipeline input and stay in attempts/<size>/.
/db/d1.sql
/db/d1.sqlite
# Pinned baseline snapshots of the attempts DB, named by their sha256 prefix.
# docs/mirt_benchmark.md's held-out sets are DRAWN from this DB, so a
# pipeline.fetch_database changes the universe, changes the draw, and voids
# every comparison recorded there. These copies are the only way back to a
# baseline; they are large binaries and never belong in git.
/db/d1.baseline-*.sqlite
# SQLite's own write-ahead-log sidecars, which exist only while a connection is
# open — a `git status` that catches them mid-query would otherwise offer to
# commit the engine's scratch space.
/db/d1.sqlite-shm
/db/d1.sqlite-wal
# Timestamped pre-change backups of the production DB (manual `cp` snapshots);
# local insurance only, never committed.
/db/d1.backup-*.sqlite

# Rating pipeline output (pipeline.train_rating / map_rating_to_egf): regenerable,
# and model.pt is a multi-MB binary. The training code is tracked; its outputs
# are not (mirrors how the heavy inputs stay but packaged outputs don't).
/ratings/

# Android TWA wrapper (Bubblewrap). Tracked: twa-manifest.json, README.md, and
# the HAND-MAINTAINED app pieces — build.gradle (version bumps), the Java
# sources (NotificationPermissionBridgeActivity is not regenerable), and
# AndroidManifest.xml (carries the bridge's activity entry). `bubblewrap
# update` would clobber those; see android/README.md before regenerating.
# Everything else (res/ icons, gradle wrapper, build outputs, store_icon.png)
# is regenerated, and android.keystore is the Play upload signing key —
# NEVER commit it. Back the keystore + its passwords up outside git (a lost
# upload key is recoverable through Play App Signing; a leaked one is not).
/android/*
!/android/twa-manifest.json
!/android/README.md
!/android/package.json
!/android/package-lock.json
!/android/app/
/android/app/*
!/android/app/build.gradle
!/android/app/src/
/android/app/src/*
!/android/app/src/main/
/android/app/src/main/*
!/android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
!/android/app/src/main/java/
# The Play listing translations are authored content (not regenerable);
# the screenshot assets around them are regenerated by
# pwa/scripts/store-screenshots.mjs and stay ignored.
!/android/store-assets/
/android/store-assets/*
!/android/store-assets/listing-texts.md

# Vast.ai orchestration
orchestration_state.json
.env

# Generated inspection/analysis artifacts (regenerable from the auxiliary/*.py
# tools): the combined PC gallery, plotted figures, and the labeler's feature
# cache. The source scripts are tracked; their outputs are not.
/all_pcs.html
auxiliary/*.png
auxiliary/labeler/cache/
# Joint rank-model placement dumps (~MBs each): regenerable from
# auxiliary/joint_rank_model*.py. The small rank_map_v2*.json result files are
# tracked; the bulky per-problem CSVs are not.
auxiliary/joint_rank_placements*.csv
# Per-user table dumped by auxiliary/time_value_analysis.py (regenerable).
auxiliary/time_value_per_user.csv
# Per-user table dumped by auxiliary/analyze_time_benefit.py (regenerable).
auxiliary/time_benefit_users.csv

# Rating-model experiment artifacts (4PL investigation; full-run outputs per config)
/ratings_*/
/experiments_4pl/*.log

# Raw user data pulled for analysis: localStorage dumps contain the user's
# account credential (userId) and full history — never version-control these.
# Called data/test/ until 2026-08-03, which read as disposable fixtures rather
# than real user data. The rest of data/ (the tracked reference inputs under
# data/reference/, and the audit outputs) is version-controlled.
/data/user_dumps/
/data/test/

# Dev experiment outputs: parameter dumps, per-run logs and the inspection HTML
# from auxiliary/rating_dev arms. Multi-hundred-MB, reproducible from the
# committed code plus a seed, and superseded as soon as the next arm runs.
dev_runs/
